# Copy this file to .env and fill it in. Never commit .env.

# A DeepSeek API key. Create one at https://platform.deepseek.com
# Use a dedicated key for this bridge so it can be revoked independently.
DEEPSEEK_API_KEY=sk-your-key-here

# Optional overrides
# DEEPSEEK_BASE_URL=https://api.deepseek.com
# DEEPSEEK_MODEL=deepseek-flash
# DEEPSEEK_TIMEOUT_MS=180000
# DEEPSEEK_MAX_OUTPUT_TOKENS=16384
#
# Note: deepseek-flash is a reasoning model, and its thinking and its answer come
# out of the SAME max_tokens budget. At 4096 a routine adversarial review spent
# the whole budget reasoning and returned an empty answer, which reached the
# caller as "no result" rather than as a truncation. 16384 is what measured out.
# Reasoning runs at roughly 165-210 tokens/s, so the full budget is 80-100 s of
# wall clock and the timeout has to sit above it. The caller's own tool-call
# window (~60 s for ChatGPT) is the tighter bound — see the README.

# Secret path segment for the MCP endpoint. Generate with:
#   npm run ctl -- secret
# The full endpoint looks like https://<your-domain>/mcp/<this value>.
MCP_PATH_SECRET=replace-with-32-byte-random-hex

PORT=8787
# Listen address. Do NOT set this to 0.0.0.0 — the tunnel runs on the same
# machine, so there is no reason to expose the port to your LAN.
# HOST=127.0.0.1

# Which transport cloudflared uses to reach the Cloudflare edge. Unset = let it
# probe (recommended). Only pin it when the probe picks wrong:
#   quic  = UDP 7844. **Use this behind a proxy / TUN-mode VPN** — TUN swallows
#           outbound TCP/7844 while UDP passes straight through. cloudflared's
#           own pre-check says `TCP Connectivity FAIL` and `UDP Connectivity PASS`.
#   http2 = TCP 7844. For a direct connection with no TUN.
# The wrong choice is a quiet failure: the process is alive and `ctl status`
# reports "running", but nothing is reachable and the log repeats
# `TLS handshake with edge error: EOF`.
# Applies to both tunnel modes.
# TUNNEL_PROTOCOL=quic

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Tunnel mode — whether the public URL changes on every restart
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# quick (default): a Cloudflare quick tunnel. No configuration at all, but each
#   start is handed a random *.trycloudflare.com hostname, so every restart
#   means rebuilding the connector in ChatGPT — and once Cloudflare recycles the
#   hostname you get `Unauthorized: Tunnel not found`.
#
# named: a fixed tunnel on **your own** domain. The URL never changes, so the
#   ChatGPT connector is created once. The cost is that you need a domain whose
#   NS already points at Cloudflare, plus a tunnel created in their dashboard.
#
# Do not commit TUNNEL_MODE=named together with the values below — TUNNEL_TOKEN
# is a full credential; anyone holding it can open a tunnel into this machine.
# (.gitignore already covers .env; this is just a reminder not to paste it
# elsewhere.)
#
# TUNNEL_MODE=quick

# Required in named mode: your public hostname. No https://, no path.
# It must already be on Cloudflare, or the dashboard cannot add a Public
# Hostname for it.
# TUNNEL_HOSTNAME=mcp.example.com

# Credential for named mode — pick one:
#
#   TUNNEL_TOKEN — the string the Cloudflare dashboard hands you when you create
#     the tunnel (roughly 150+ characters). Recommended: no login, no cert.pem,
#     just paste. Don't type it into the file by hand — run
#     `npm run ctl -- tunnel named <your-hostname>` and it will ask for the token
#     and write it for you. Hand-copying a long string drops characters, and a
#     token that is one character short fails in a way that looks nothing like a
#     typo.
#   TUNNEL_NAME  — if you created the tunnel with the cloudflared CLI
#     (login/create/route dns), put the tunnel name here. On that path
#     cloudflared reads its own config and credentials, so the routing rule
#     lives in **that** config, not in TUNNEL_HOSTNAME — keep the two in step.
#
# TUNNEL_TOKEN=
# TUNNEL_NAME=

# Once configured:
#   npm run ctl -- tunnel check      validate the config, and say what Cloudflare
#                                    still needs
#   npm run ctl -- untunnel && npm run tunnel
#
# ⚠️ The trap in named mode is forgetting the route in the dashboard
#    The tunnel then reports "connected to the edge" and everything locally looks
#    healthy, but the domain returns 404 — because the "hostname → local port"
#    route lives in Cloudflare's cloud, not on this machine.
#    Dashboard path: Networking → Tunnels → your tunnel → Routes → Add route
#    → Published application, and add an HTTP route to http://localhost:8787

# Requests per minute. **One global bucket, not per IP**: the server listens on
# loopback and the only thing reaching it is the tunnel, so X-Forwarded-For is
# whatever the caller typed. Trusting it makes the limit read as "N/min per
# made-up IP".
# Only tools/call is counted — not the handshake or tools/list. In stateless
# mode a single poll can cost three requests, so counting those would let a long
# job 429 itself with its own polling. 20 was small enough for one working
# sub-agent to hit.
RATE_LIMIT_PER_MINUTE=60

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Agent jobs — read the Security model section of README.md before setting any
# of these. Leaving this block commented out keeps the bridge read-only.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Roots a sub-agent may work in, separated by ';'. EMPTY MEANS DENY EVERYTHING.
# Setting this is the switch that hands over the machine: whoever holds the MCP
# URL can then make the sub-agent read, write and run commands in those roots.
# DEEPSEEK_ALLOWED_ROOTS=D:\work\agent-scratch

# Step ceiling per job (one tool call = one step).
# 40 was too low: a read-only review that walks a whole project passes it easily
# and gets cut off just short of the end. A job that does hit the ceiling still
# stops, but what it had already read comes back as a partial result rather than
# nothing.
# BRIDGE_MAX_STEPS=120

# Wall-clock ceiling per job, ms. On expiry the job and its whole process tree
# are killed. Keep this below the registry's own hard wall (server.ts sets that
# to this value + 1 minute); if the hard wall fires first it reports the stop as
# a plain cancellation, which hides the real reason.
# BRIDGE_JOB_TIMEOUT_MS=1800000

# How long a command waits for a human, ms. Expiry is a deny.
# BRIDGE_APPROVAL_TIMEOUT_MS=300000

# Command NAMES that run without asking. Anything else pauses the job until you
# answer with `npm run ctl -- approve <id>` / `deny <id>`.
# The default list is the one below plus PowerShell's read-only cmdlets
# (Get-Content / Get-ChildItem / Test-Path / Select-String / Get-FileHash …);
# the full list is in src/agent/approvals.ts.
# Note that this covers **commands only**: file reads and writes outside the
# workspace, and any network tool, always go to a human.
# BRIDGE_APPROVE_ALLOW=node,npm,npx,git,tsc,dir,ls,cat,type,find,grep,echo

# Set to "off" to skip the approval queue entirely. ⚠️ What it removes is more
# than "the asking about commands": the approval-mcp.ts child process is never
# spawned at all, so the command allowlist, the chaining guard (&&, |, ;) and
# the file tools' workspace boundary **all** disappear together.
# (DEEPSEEK_ALLOWED_ROOTS still applies — it governs which directories may be
# opened as a workspace in the first place.)
#
# ⚠️ This one does **not** reset on restart; it needs `npm run ctl -- reload`.
# That is exactly why it is a poor everyday switch — for a temporary bypass use
# `npm run auto:on` (below). Leave this commented; only `npm run accept`
# overrides it explicitly.
# BRIDGE_CC_APPROVAL=off

# The temporary bypass is not written to this file — it is the flag file
# `.state/auto-approve`:
#   npm run auto:on    fully unattended; takes effect on the NEXT job, no restart
#   npm run auto:off   back to "needs approval"
#   npm run auto       show the current mode
# It only lasts as long as the service runs: restarting revokes it automatically.
# That is deliberate — the bypass means "I am watching this one right now", not
# "I have stopped caring".

# Path to the Claude Code binary, if not on PATH.
# BRIDGE_CLAUDE_BIN=

# Where the harness sends its requests. Defaults to DEEPSEEK_BASE_URL + /anthropic.
# BRIDGE_ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL=

# Job snapshots, approval queue and audit log live here.
# BRIDGE_STATE_DIR=

# Injected by the harness, one value per job — it is how the approval server
# learns the boundary it is enforcing. Do not set it by hand.
# BRIDGE_WORKSPACE=

# Opt-in --max-budget-usd for the harness. Off by default because Claude Code
# prices at Claude rates: a limit here reads as roughly 100x the real DeepSeek
# cost and would cut jobs off early.
# BRIDGE_CC_MAX_BUDGET_USD=
